<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Is each of these a vector quantity or a scalar quantity?</p><div>(a) the weight of a sack of maize, 900 N: [ a vector quantity | a scalar quantity ]  </div><div>(b) a push of 200 N on a handcart, towards the east: [ a vector quantity | a scalar quantity ]  </div><div>(c) the price of a bunch of bananas, Ksh 120: [ a vector quantity | a scalar quantity ]  </div><div>(d) the velocity of a train travelling north at 80 km/h: [ a vector quantity | a scalar quantity ]  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>A scalar has a size and a unit, nothing more. A vector has a size and a direction.</p><ul><li>(a) the weight of a sack of maize, 900 N: a vector quantity. Weight is a force, the pull of the earth, and it always acts downwards, so it is a vector even when no direction is written.</li><li>(b) a push of 200 N on a handcart, towards the east: a vector quantity. The direction is part of this quantity: it is a vector.</li><li>(c) the price of a bunch of bananas, Ksh 120: a scalar quantity. This has a size and a unit but no direction: a scalar.</li><li>(d) the velocity of a train travelling north at 80 km/h: a vector quantity. A velocity has a direction (here north): it is a vector.</li></ul></p></solution>
    <answer><p>a vector quantity</p></answer>
    <answer><p>a vector quantity</p></answer>
    <answer><p>a scalar quantity</p></answer>
    <answer><p>a vector quantity</p></answer>
</stack-static>